package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part uplifting community story. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch39 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_window_with_the_green_light",
            title = "The Window with the Green Light",
            tagline = "Two students uncover the real story behind a glowing window and help a neighbourhood learning room reopen.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "The Window with the Green Light", listOf(
                    "When Nia started helping at the neighbourhood library on Saturdays, she expected to sort returned books and place labels on new shelves. Instead, the librarian, Mr Ade, asked her to help investigate a small mystery. Across the road stood an old two-storey building that had once housed a community learning centre. Most of its windows were dark, but every evening a green light appeared in one upstairs room. Nobody seemed to know who switched it on. Some people thought it was an old timer, others believed the building had been rented, and a few had invented stories about secret meetings. Mr Ade did not want rumours repeated as facts. He asked Nia to help find out what the building was actually used for, beginning with records the library was allowed to share.",
                    "Nia invited her classmate Tobi to help. They began with the library's local-history shelf, where a thin booklet described the learning centre's opening twenty-five years earlier. It had offered evening lessons, reading groups, and practical workshops for adults who could not attend during the day. A photograph showed a green lamp in the upstairs window, placed there so late visitors could find the entrance. The booklet did not say whether the centre still operated. Tobi noticed that the photograph had a date written on the back, while the booklet's final page listed a committee meeting several years later. They copied the public references into their notebook and agreed to ask the librarian before contacting anyone mentioned in the records.",
                    "Mr Ade helped them examine old public notices. The most recent one announced that the centre had closed temporarily for repairs, but it gave no reopening date. Nia wondered whether the green light had simply been left on. Mr Ade said that was possible, but the building had an electricity meter and a caretaker, so someone might still be using part of it. He reminded them not to enter the property or look through windows. Their investigation had to respect privacy and safety. The next step was straightforward: ask the building's current owner or caretaker whether the light was intentional. Nia wrote the question carefully, because she wanted an answer rather than a more exciting rumour."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Caretaker's Key", listOf(
                    "The library sent a polite letter to the building manager using the public contact address on an old notice. Three days later, a reply arrived from Mr Ekanem, the caretaker. He explained that the green lamp was switched on each evening because the upstairs room was being prepared for a new reading project. The centre had not reopened yet, but a small committee was restoring the space and checking which activities residents wanted. He invited the students to meet him at the library, where he could show them a few public documents. Nia was pleased that the mystery had a sensible answer. Tobi, however, wondered why the committee had not placed a notice outside the building. Mr Ekanem said they were still waiting for permission to use the front noticeboard.",
                    "At the meeting, Mr Ekanem brought a folder containing a building inspection summary, a list of completed repairs, and a draft timetable. He did not share personal information about volunteers or residents. The students learned that the roof had been repaired and the upstairs room had passed its inspection, but the ground-floor entrance still needed a handrail. The committee planned to offer a quiet reading hour, homework support, and a weekly skills exchange. The green lamp was a temporary sign that work was continuing, not an invitation to enter. Nia asked whether the committee needed help making the plans easier to understand. Mr Ekanem said a clear public notice would be useful once the dates were confirmed.",
                    "Tobi suggested that they design a poster immediately. Nia reminded him that the timetable was still a draft. They could prepare a layout with blank spaces, but they should not publish dates that might change. Mr Ekanem liked the idea and gave them the approved project name: The Open Window Room. The committee would review the poster before it appeared outside. As they left, Nia looked across the road at the upstairs window. The green light no longer seemed mysterious. It was a small signal of work being done carefully, even when the finished project was not ready to welcome visitors."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "A List of Unanswered Questions", listOf(
                    "The students returned to the library with a clearer picture of the project. Mr Ade helped them make a list of what was confirmed and what remained undecided. The room would have reading activities, supervised study time, and skill-sharing sessions. The opening date was not confirmed. The entrance handrail was still being installed, and the committee had not decided how many people could use the room at once. Nia suggested that the poster include a line saying that details would be announced after the final inspection. Tobi wanted to add a countdown, but they agreed that a countdown would create an expectation the committee might not be able to meet. They prepared a draft with simple headings and enough space for updates.",
                    "While checking the old booklet again, Nia noticed a page describing a community project called One Skill, One Hour. Residents had once offered short lessons in sewing, basic computer use, gardening, and reading aloud. The idea had been to share practical knowledge without charging a fee. Tobi thought it sounded like a collection of classes, but the booklet explained that participants could teach something small or simply attend and learn. No one was expected to be an expert. Nia wondered whether the new room might revive the idea. She wrote it down as a suggestion, not as a decision, and asked Mr Ekanem whether the committee had seen the old programme.",
                    "Mr Ekanem replied that the committee had found the same page and was considering a modern version. They were careful not to promise that every old activity would return. Some equipment was no longer available, and the volunteers who had led earlier sessions might not be able to participate. The students understood that a good project needed realistic limits. They offered to help gather suggestions from library visitors using an anonymous comment sheet. Mr Ade approved, provided the questions were respectful and did not request names, addresses, or private details. Nia wrote three questions about preferred topics, convenient times, and accessibility needs. The form would help the committee plan without making anyone feel obliged to explain personal circumstances."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The Comment Box", listOf(
                    "The comment sheet appeared beside the library entrance with a clear note explaining its purpose. Visitors could leave it blank or write as much as they wanted. During the first week, Nia and Tobi received suggestions for beginner computer skills, book discussions, drawing, homework support, and a quiet place to read. Several people asked for activities on Saturday mornings. Others preferred early evenings. A few requested large-print materials and a step-free entrance. The students grouped the answers by topic rather than trying to identify who had written them. Mr Ade showed them how to count responses without treating a small sample as proof of what everyone wanted. The results were useful, but they were only a starting point.",
                    "One comment asked for a place where people could practise speaking in front of a small group without being laughed at. Tobi thought this might fit the skills exchange. Nia suggested a low-pressure storytelling circle where participants could read a paragraph, share a favourite book, or simply listen. They included the idea in the summary, along with a reminder that listening should count as participation. Another comment asked whether visitors could borrow materials from the room. Mr Ade explained that the committee would need a simple borrowing system and a record of which items belonged to the library. The students added this as a practical question for the next meeting.",
                    "When the committee reviewed the summary, it thanked the students but asked them to avoid describing the comments as a vote. The forms had been available only at one library, so the responses could not represent every resident. Nia agreed and changed the heading from What the Neighbourhood Wants to Suggestions from Library Visitors. The difference mattered. A useful report did not make its evidence sound larger than it was. The committee decided to hold an open planning afternoon after the entrance work was complete. The students offered to display the suggestions and help visitors add ideas in person."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Day the Light Went Out", listOf(
                    "One rainy Tuesday, the green light did not appear in the upstairs window. Tobi noticed first and sent Nia a message asking whether the project had stopped. She reminded him that a lamp could be switched off for many ordinary reasons. The next morning, Mr Ekanem confirmed that an electrician had disconnected it while checking a circuit. The inspection had found no serious problem, but the committee wanted the wiring tested before using the room. The light would remain off until the work was signed off. Nia was glad the caretaker had explained the change. She also realised how quickly people could turn a small unknown into a story. She and Tobi decided not to post guesses online.",
                    "The students updated the draft poster to say that the room was still being prepared and that the opening information would be shared after the inspection. They did not mention the electrical work because the committee had not asked them to publish technical details. Mr Ekanem approved the wording. Tobi suggested adding a small illustration of a lamp, but Nia proposed an open window instead. The symbol would show that the project was about welcoming people when it was ready, not about the light itself. They chose a simple drawing with a book on the sill and a plant beside it. The poster looked friendly without promising a date.",
                    "At the next library session, a younger student asked whether the green light meant there was a secret club. Nia smiled and explained what they knew: the room was being prepared for public learning activities, and the committee would announce the opening when the work was complete. She did not repeat the rumours or make fun of the question. The student asked whether children could attend. Nia said the final age arrangements had not been announced, so she could not promise anything. Mr Ade praised her for giving a clear answer and admitting what she did not know. The students added a short question-and-answer section to the poster draft, with a note that official details would come from the committee."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A Room for Different Learners", listOf(
                    "The entrance handrail was completed, and the committee invited a small group to review the room's layout. Nia and Tobi attended with Mr Ade and two adult volunteers. The upstairs room had tables, shelves, and a reading corner, but the group noticed that one shelf blocked part of the walking space. They moved it after checking with the caretaker. A volunteer suggested arranging the chairs in a circle for discussion sessions. Another recommended leaving some chairs near the wall for people who preferred not to sit in the centre. Nia liked that idea because a welcoming room should offer more than one comfortable way to participate.",
                    "They checked the signs on the shelves and discovered that some labels were too small to read from a distance. Tobi offered to redesign them with larger letters and simple symbols. The committee also planned to keep a printed activity schedule near the entrance and a digital copy on its public page. Nia asked whether the schedule could be available in a plain-text format for people who used screen readers. The volunteer responsible for communications agreed to include that option. They also discussed keeping a quiet corner free from group activities during each session. The room would not be perfect for every need, but the committee could make practical adjustments and listen when visitors suggested improvements.",
                    "During the review, a volunteer asked the students to help choose the first activity. Nia proposed a shared reading hour because it required little equipment and could welcome people with different levels of experience. Tobi suggested a simple creative workshop for the following week, but the committee decided to begin with one activity and learn from it. They wrote a plan with a clear start and finish time, a maximum group size, and an adult coordinator. The students were not asked to supervise visitors or collect personal information. Their role was to help prepare the display and welcome people alongside the responsible adults. Nia thought the plan felt modest, but it also felt possible."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The First Open Afternoon", listOf(
                    "The committee announced the first open afternoon after the final inspection was signed. The notice included the date, time, entrance information, and a contact route for questions. It explained that the first session would be a shared reading hour and that visitors could listen without reading aloud. Nia and Tobi placed copies at the library, school, and community hall after receiving permission from each location. They checked the printed versions against the committee's final text so no old draft remained on display. Mr Ade reminded them to remove the notices when the event had passed. Clear information needed to stay current, not simply look attractive.",
                    "On the afternoon itself, the room filled slowly. Some visitors brought books, while others chose from the new shelves. A retired mechanic read a short passage about learning a craft. A student shared a poem they had written, and a parent listened from a chair near the window. Nobody was pressured to speak. The coordinator introduced the room's guidelines: respect different opinions, avoid interrupting, and ask before borrowing materials. Nia helped visitors find the large-print books. Tobi showed a child where the drawing paper was kept. The open afternoon did not feel like a grand launch. It felt like a useful beginning, with enough space for people to discover what suited them.",
                    "Halfway through the session, the printed schedule fell from the noticeboard and one page was damaged by a spilled cup of water. Nia found the digital copy on the committee's public page and printed a replacement at the library desk. The coordinator checked it before displaying it. Tobi joked that the room had passed its first test of unexpected events. Nia thought the important part was that they had a backup and knew who could approve a replacement. At the end, visitors left short comments about what they enjoyed and what could be improved. The committee collected the sheets and thanked everyone. The green lamp was on again, but now the room itself was the message."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "The Story Behind the Window", listOf(
                    "After the first session, Mr Ekanem showed the students a framed photograph that the committee had decided to display. It showed the learning centre's first volunteers standing beneath the same upstairs window. The green lamp was visible behind them. On the back, a handwritten note said that the lamp had been chosen by a young learner who once struggled to find the entrance during an evening class. The volunteers had placed it in the window so newcomers would know they had reached the right room. The story was simple, but it explained why the lamp had remained part of the building's identity even after the centre closed.",
                    "Nia asked whether the learner had been named in the old records. Mr Ekanem said the note did not include a name, and the committee had decided not to guess. The person might still live nearby, but the photograph did not provide enough information to identify them. Tobi suggested writing a caption that honoured the idea without inventing a biography. They drafted: The green lamp began as a welcome sign for evening learners. It reminds us to make the entrance easy to find and the room easier to enter. The committee approved the caption. Nia liked that it respected the unknown person while keeping the useful part of the story.",
                    "The students added the photograph and caption to a small display near the window. They also included a short timeline showing the centre's opening, its temporary closure, the repair work, and the new room's first session. Each date came from a public record or an approved committee note. Where the exact date was unknown, they used a year or left the space blank. Mr Ade said that honest gaps were better than invented details. Nia realised that the investigation had taught them more than the answer to one question. It had shown them how to preserve a community story without claiming to know everything about the people who made it."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "A Plan That Can Grow", listOf(
                    "The committee met a month after the first open afternoon to review what had worked. The shared reading hour had attracted more visitors than expected, but the group size had remained manageable because the coordinator had prepared extra chairs. Several people asked for a beginner computer session, while others wanted the quiet reading hour to continue. The committee decided to keep the reading session and trial one computer workshop with a trained adult volunteer. It would not promise a permanent weekly class until the trial had been evaluated. Nia and Tobi helped prepare a feedback sheet that asked whether the instructions were clear, whether the pace felt comfortable, and what topics visitors would like next.",
                    "The computer workshop needed careful planning. The volunteer checked that the devices were updated and that no personal accounts or private files would be used during the demonstration. Participants would practise with sample documents created for the session. The committee arranged for a helper to assist anyone who wanted extra time. Nia helped make a one-page guide with large headings and simple steps. Tobi reviewed the wording and noticed that one instruction assumed everyone already knew how to open a folder. They added an explanation and a small diagram. The students were learning that good teaching did not show off how much the teacher knew; it made the next step understandable to the learner.",
                    "At the end of the trial, the committee reviewed the feedback and decided to offer another workshop with a slower pace and a short break in the middle. The quiet reading hour would remain on the calendar. Other ideas would be considered when volunteers and space were available. Nia and Tobi were pleased that the project was growing without pretending it could do everything at once. They had seen how a small room could become useful through clear plans, honest limits, and attention to the people who used it. The green lamp still shone in the upstairs window each evening, not as a puzzle to solve, but as a steady sign that the room was open when its doors were scheduled to welcome visitors."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "The Window Stays Open", listOf(
                    "At the end of the school term, Nia's media club prepared a short presentation about the Open Window Room. They included the original question, the records they checked, the people who helped, and the practical changes that followed. They were careful not to describe the building as a secret centre or claim that every neighbourhood resident used it. Instead, they explained how the committee had restored one room and started a small programme based on community suggestions. The presentation included the old photograph, the approved caption, and the current activity notice. Mr Ade reviewed the slides and asked them to add the date when the information had last been checked. Nia added it beneath the timetable.",
                    "During the presentation, a student asked whether the green lamp would ever be replaced. Tobi answered that the committee could choose to change it, but the story behind it would remain in the archive. Another student asked whether the room could host music lessons, sports meetings, and every other suggestion at once. Nia explained that the committee would have to consider space, equipment, volunteers, and accessibility before adding activities. The answer did not sound as exciting as saying yes to everything, but it was honest. Their teacher said that a project became trustworthy when its promises matched what its organisers could actually provide.",
                    "On their final Saturday of the term, Nia and Tobi visited the room as participants rather than helpers. They chose books, sat near the window, and listened while a volunteer read a chapter aloud. The green lamp cast a soft circle on the sill. Nia thought back to the first evening when she had seen it and wondered who was behind the light. Now she knew the answer, but the best discovery was not the identity of a person or the explanation of a switch. It was the way a question had led them to records, conversations, careful planning, and a place where people could learn together. Before leaving, she wrote a note in the club journal: A light can help someone find a door. A welcoming community makes sure the door is ready to open."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
